ssion is consistent with blast crisis phase of CML, though the high blast count is more typical of AML. This maintains moderate support for B1's myeloid neoplasm category."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the blast morphology features (35% blasts) consistent with lymphoid neoplasm like ALL?", "summary": "35% blasts are less typical of ALL (which usually shows higher blast counts in marrow), but peripheral blast presence still provides some support for lymphoid neoplasms."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute onset (several days) with sudden visual acuity changes and ataxia argue against AML as the primary diagnosis?", "summary": "AML can present with abrupt neurological changes via leukemic infiltration, though this is less common than constitutional symptoms alone. Does not strongly contradict B1."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the 10-lb weight loss and fever over several days with acute neurological symptoms disfavor chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms?", "summary": "These findings argue against typical chronic phase CML/MPN presentations, which usually have more gradual symptom development."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of lymphadenopathy or skin findings in the exam weaken the lymphoid neoplasm hypothesis?", "summary": "Lack of lymphadenopathy reduces support for ALL, which commonly presents with lymphadenopathy and skin involvement."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the constitutional symptoms and high blast count more consistent with multiple myeloma or a myeloid neoplasm?", "summary": "Constitutional symptoms and high blast count are more characteristic of myeloid neoplasms than plasma cell disorders like multiple myeloma."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B2", 0.558], ["B3", 0.178], ["B4", 0.117]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_